export const summaries21to30: Record<number, { zh: string; en: string }> = {
  21: {
    zh: `魏聘才在华府住了月余，上下周旋得法，又蒙华公子看顾，银钱宽展，便渐渐小人得志：内有顾月卿、张笑梅，外有杨梅窗、冯子佩，朝欢暮乐，所见所闻无非钻营势利，遂心肥胆大。从前在梅宅有梅士燮在家自然畏惧，梅子玉所交又都是名士，如马困槽枥不能泛驾；如今却是任意所欲，无所忌惮。一日他雇了十三太保玻璃热车，把四儿也打扮起来，意气扬扬出城探病。子玉病了月余，意懒神疲，饮食大减，颜夫人只当他读书伤了心血，参苓调理总不见效——原来这是七情所感，草木无从解劝，除非琴言亲来畅谈一昼夜方可霍然。聘才把华公子、林珊枝与八龄班夸得上天下地，又说连琴言也不及；子玉一听便动了心事，问他既亲见其人绝世无双，何以又说有多少胜他的。聘才便打比方：琴言的相貌虽好，性情过冷，如高枝之花攀折不到，如冬夜之月寒到肌骨，不如近手的春花、三秋的明月又好看又不冷。子玉却正色驳他：花无人折则自保其芳，月无人看则更形其皎；难攀之花遇珍禽仙露愈显其好，寒天之月映寒梅白雪愈见其清；琴言的生香活色人所能知，而挚意深情人罕能喻——一席话无意间竟把聘才归入了"俗人异趣"，聘才心上不悦，只得勉强称是。子玉又托他带话：切莫说我有病，只说不要紧，几天就好；又劝琴言常找陆素兰谈谈，郁闷一开自然痊愈。聘才辞出，暗想庾香既爱其人何不常叫来畅叙，何必闷出病来，径往樱桃巷而去。恰值长庆出门，他直闯进去：小院无人，绿窗深闭，庭前一树黄梅红绽半边，檐下白鹦鹉忽然叫道"客来了，莫要进来"。房中一股幽香药味，壁上悬着萧次贤画的四幅红梅与徐子云所书四幅篆字，炕几胆瓶里插一枝梅花，琴桌上横着一张梅花断纹的瑶琴，连内房软帘上绣的也是各色梅花。琴言穿着湖色纺绸夹袄出来，比前消瘦，正似雪里梅花。他听得"出城"二字，只当子玉搬了家，忙问搬在何处；聘才见他问起庾香便高兴、对自己却冰冷，心中可恶，索性捏造起来：先冷笑说庾香近来不记得你了，继而绘声绘色说今日进去时听见笑声，云儿高喊"有客来了"，庾香卧在床上满脸通红、答非所问，帐后有响动，他借捡扇子揭帘一看，见一双靴子与白花绉绸的衫子边儿，出门时又见蓉官的跟人在门房说话，因此断定是蓉官；末了还叹惋他撇了旧情，玷污了干净身子。琴言听得心酸落泪，虽半信半疑，却又忍不住追问；聘才再进一步，说他既与庾香相好就不该常在怡园，难怪人家有醋意，还说那张琴也已转送了人。琴言又羞又气，几乎哭出来，起身入内，吩咐小使请魏少爷回府。聘才大怒，转念又怕糟蹋了庾香的心上人反招见怪，只忿忿上车，暗誓要慢慢收拾他。琴言卧床垂泪，左思右想不能明白，忽记起素兰那日的话未曾说完，便遣人去请。素兰一到，先笑他除了庾香再无事寻我，随即说出实情：前日怡园看龙舟，度香请庾香而他因病未到，说他病了一个多月，脸上清瘦了好些，十天前还到过怡园；又讲一件笑话——那日萧次贤把田春航替苏蕙芳做的小序给庾香看，度香在旁赞"愈艳愈好，愈浓愈好"，庾香竟误听作问他玉侬与玉艳孰优，脱口答道"玉侬自然在玉艳之上，玉艳虽好尚逊瑶卿、媚香一筹，而玉侬则玉树琼花，非人间花谱可以位置"，说得度香、次贤大笑，他自己也窘得支吾遮饰。琴言听到此再忍不住，泪如线穿，把胸前一片纱都湿透了，又顿足痛骂魏聘才无缘无故造言生事。素兰劝住了，又说些外间新闻：那日行酒令时连尖利的媚香也吃了闷亏；媚香为潘其观纠缠不清，已躲到湘帆处几天，如今吩咐下人只回不在家，又在床后开了一个通厨房的门以备躲避。素兰再讲一桩：春阳馆的东家因不认得一位姓魏的师爷，写账时得罪了他，那人醉后摔笔推砚砸碎一柜家伙，写票子时写的是"华公府师老爷"；次日兵马司一枝火签一条链子把掌柜的锁去，亏得求了张仲雨花几十吊讲情，只打了二十嘴巴才放出来，还送了两桌酒席。琴言道，管他是不是，横竖叫魏聘才的总不是好东西。素兰又笑他满屋满身都是梅花，是睹物怀人；却不知庾香身上的荷包、扇络无一不是琴的样式，连扇面画的也是一正一反两张琴——说得琴言又哭了。临别素兰嘱他：这人既进了华公府，小人得志必要作威作福，恶人自有造祸之才，不可不防；又说过几天想个法子，请庾香来会他一面。`,
    en: `A month and more in the Hua mansion, courting everyone high and low and enjoying both his master's favour and a fuller purse, Wei Pincai begins to swell. Indoors he has Gu Yueqing and Zhang Xiaomei, outdoors Yang Meichuang and Feng Zipei, and a daily round of pleasure in which everything seen and heard concerns influence and advantage. At the Mei house, with Mei Shixie present, he had been kept in check, and Mei Ziyu's friends were all scholars and gentlemen — a horse penned at the manger cannot bolt. Now nothing restrains him. One day he hires a fashionable glazed carriage, dresses up Si'er, and rides out grandly to visit the invalid. Ziyu has been ill above a month, listless and off his food; Lady Yan supposes hard study has drained him and doses him with ginseng to no effect, for the sickness comes of feeling, which no root or bark can argue with, and nothing would cure it but a day and a night of talk with Qinyan. Pincai praises Hua Guangsu, Lin Shanzhi, and the boys' troupe to the skies, adding that even Qinyan does not equal them — which touches Ziyu at once, and he asks how a man who has seen that peerless face can speak so. Pincai offers his comparison: Qinyan's looks are fine but his nature is cold, a flower on a branch too high to pluck, a winter moon whose brilliance chills the bone, less lovable than blossom within reach or the mild moon of autumn. Ziyu answers gravely that a flower no one plucks keeps its own fragrance, and a moon no one watches shines the whiter; that a flower beyond reach shows best among rare birds and immortal dew, and a winter moon best above plum and snow; that Qinyan's living beauty anyone may see, but his depth of feeling few can construe. Without meaning to, he classes his guest among the vulgar, and Pincai, stung, can only agree. Ziyu entrusts him with a message: say nothing of my illness, or if he asks, say it is slight; and tell him to talk often with Lu Sulan, since lifting the gloom will speed his recovery. Riding away, Pincai reflects that a man who loves someone should simply send for him rather than sicken in silence, and goes straight to Cherry Blossom Lane. Changqing is out, so he walks in: an empty courtyard, green windows shut, a plum tree hung with yellow fruit half flushed red, and under the eaves a white parrot that suddenly announces, "A guest has come — do not go in." The room smells of medicine and faint perfume; on the walls hang four red-plum paintings by Xiao Cixian and four sheets of seal script by Xu Ziyun; a plum spray stands in a vase, a qin with plum-bark cracks lies on the table, and even the inner curtain is embroidered with plum. Qinyan comes out in a lake-blue jacket, thinner than before, like plum in snow. Hearing the words "outside the city," he takes it that Ziyu has moved and asks anxiously where. Pincai, nettled that the boy brightens at Ziyu's name and freezes at his own, begins to invent. He laughs coldly and says Ziyu no longer remembers him; then, pressed, describes arriving to hear laughter, Yun'er calling out loudly that a guest had come, Ziyu flushed on his bed and answering at random, a stirring behind the curtain, and how, stooping for a dropped fan, he glimpsed a pair of boots and the hem of a white crepe gown — and how, leaving, he saw Rongguan's servant gossiping at the gatehouse. He ends by lamenting that Ziyu should throw away such devotion and soil himself so. Qinyan's tears fall though he half disbelieves, and he cannot stop questioning. Pincai presses further: since Qinyan is so much at the Yi Garden, no wonder there is jealousy — and adds that the qin has been given away to someone else. At that Qinyan rises, wounded and furious, goes into the inner room, and sends word that the gentleman may return home. Pincai leaves in a rage, restrained only by the thought that harming Ziyu's beloved would earn Ziyu's anger, and swears to settle the account by degrees. Weeping on his bed, Qinyan remembers that Sulan's account was once cut short and sends for him. Sulan arrives, teases that Qinyan never wants him except about Ziyu, and then tells the truth: at the dragon-boat party Duxiang had invited Ziyu, who was too ill to come, having been sick above a month and grown thin, though he had called at the garden ten days before. And there was a joke — Xiao Cixian showed Ziyu the preface Tian Chunhang wrote for Su Huifang's birthday, and when Duxiang praised it as the richer the better, the more voluptuous the better, Ziyu misheard it as a question about which he preferred, Yunong or Yuyan, and blurted out that Yunong naturally stands above Yuyan, that Yuyan for all his merit falls short of Yaoqing and Meixiang, and that Yunong is a jade tree and a flower of heaven not to be placed in any earthly register at all — at which the two men laughed and Ziyu covered his confusion badly. Qinyan weeps until the gauze at his breast is soaked, then stamps and curses Wei Pincai for inventing slanders out of nothing. Sulan calms him with other news: at the drinking game even the sharp-witted Meixiang was caught out; and Meixiang, harried by Pan Qiguan, has hidden several days at Xiangfan's, now instructs his servants to say he is never at home, and has had a door cut behind his bed into the kitchen for escape. Then Sulan tells another story: the manager of the Chunyang Restaurant, not recognising a certain Master Wei, offended him over the writing of a bill; the man, drunk, threw down the brush, shoved the great inkstone off the counter and smashed a whole set of ware, and signed the note in the name of the Hua mansion. Next morning the ward office came with a warrant and a chain; only by begging Zhang Zhongyu and spending tens of strings did the manager escape with twenty blows on the face — and two banquets besides. Qinyan says it hardly matters whether it is the same man, since anyone called Wei Pincai is certain to be a scoundrel. Sulan then laughs that Qinyan's room and clothes are all plum, an object standing for a person — and adds that Ziyu's purse and fan-cords are all shaped like a qin, and his fan painted with two of them, one upright and one reversed; at which Qinyan weeps again. Leaving, Sulan warns him that a mean man newly in power will certainly abuse it, that wickedness has a talent for making trouble, and that Qinyan must be on his guard — and promises that in a few days he will contrive a meeting with Ziyu.`,
  },
  22: {
    zh: `陆素兰被琴言催得无计，寓中人杂，酒楼又不便倾诉，终于想出泛舟运河之计：在大东门外雇一只精洁画船，搬去自家玩器笔砚花卉，只约琴言一人，再借徐子云名义修书请梅子玉。子玉正对着子云所赠瑶琴消磨长日，见封面字迹既不像子云，也不像萧次贤，拆开才知是素兰相招，且暗许一见玉侬，一时心花大开，病去九分，即刻回帖应约。素兰持信去报喜，才进二门便听见一片喝骂：两个凶汉捶台拍桌，骂琴言不过一个小旦，装病摆架子，扬言要毁他的窝、揍他师傅曹长庆；家人低声下气，奉茶装烟，直送出四吊钱才把这两尊瘟神劝走。素兰隔窗偷看被瞧见，几乎被追上。原来一个是华光宿府中的车夫，一个是官厨小子，都是魏聘才花八吊钱买来的。素兰奔到琴言房中，蓝纱帐里一片呜咽。琴言说出死志：求他转求徐子云，把自己葬在怡园梅崦的梅树下；再不然烧成灰，往山高水深处顺风吹散，省得留一个苦命的痕迹在人间。才十六岁，已活得不耐烦了。二人对哭半个时辰，素兰自伤才貌之人天所必妒，转而察出闹事者的来路，琴言方恍然大悟：魏聘才造谣于前、遣人于后。素兰以子玉的回信解劝，琴言看了又看，才微微一笑。是夜四更风雨雷电大作，琴言梦见刚要上船便被恶汉揪住，惊出一身冷汗；醒来又听檐下鹦鹉学舌道「昨日的人又来了」。次日子玉冒着残雨早起，捡出江绸湖绸、零碎玩物与十几两碎银预备酬谢素兰，却被赤脚披衫的李元茂缠住借钱，误了时辰，直到午初才赶到船上。素兰有意相试，先说玉侬病重不能来，子玉如冰水淋身，只叹好事多磨；素兰又说另有一个貌似玉侬的可作替身，子玉正色不肯，道当日怡园已被唐突过一次，何必再叫婢学夫人。话未了，后舱转出一个如花似玉的人来——正是琴言，比前清减，从前国色天姿，如今却落花无言、人淡如菊。三人抱头痛哭，然后说破魏聘才的种种阴谋，子玉气得神色惨淡，誓与之绝交。酒摆上来，愁云渐散：一个看对面是瑶柯琪树、秋月冰壶，一个看对面是蔷薇濯露、芍药笼烟，只恨鲁阳无戈，留不住那一轮红日。忽有一船横过，急管繁弦，三个袒裼的汉子怀抱小旦对口喝酒——素兰指出正是缠扰苏蕙芳的潘其观，怀里坐的是登春班的玉美。一水之隔，两种"乐"字相照：这边是相对忘言的清愁，那边是狂笑如奔流的浊欢。日落催归，子玉分赠礼物、散赏跟人，在大东门执手而别，琴言道往后相见不在香畹处便在怡园。子玉回家已上灯，颜夫人问起，只得随口支吾。`,
    en: `Pressed by Qinyan day after day, Lu Sulan can find nowhere private enough: his own lodging is too crowded, a restaurant too public for hearts to be emptied. At last he hits on a day's drifting up the canal. He hires a trim painted boat outside the Great East Gate, moves aboard his own curios, brushes, inkstones and flowering plants, invites Qinyan alone, and writes to Mei Ziyu in Xu Ziyun's name. Ziyu, wearing out the long day over the qin Ziyun gave him, notices that the hand on the envelope is neither Ziyun's nor Xiao Cixian's; opening it, he finds Sulan's invitation with its veiled promise of Yunong, and nine-tenths of his illness lifts on the spot. He answers at once that he will certainly come. Sulan carries the reply to Qinyan's house and, barely inside the second gate, hears cursing: two bruisers pounding the table, jeering that Qinyan is only a boy actor giving himself airs, swearing to wreck the place and thrash his master Cao Changqing. The servants bow, pour tea, pack the water-pipe, and finally buy them off with four strings of cash. Sulan, spying through a window crack, is seen and barely outruns them. The pair, it emerges, are a carriage driver from Hua Guangsu's mansion and a boy from the official kitchen, hired by Wei Pincai for eight strings. Sulan runs to Qinyan and finds him sobbing behind blue gauze curtains. Qinyan speaks of dying: let Sulan beg Xu Ziyun to bury his bones beneath the plum trees of the Plum Slope at Yiyuan, or else burn him and scatter the ash on the wind where the mountains are high and the water deep, so that no trace of an unlucky life remains to trouble anyone. He is sixteen, he says, and already weary of living. They weep together half an hour; Sulan reflects that heaven envies whoever is given beauty and talent, then reasons out where the ruffians came from, and Qinyan sees the whole design at last — Wei Pincai's slanders first, his hired men after. Sulan quiets him with Ziyu's letter, which Qinyan reads and rereads until he smiles faintly. At the fourth watch wind, rain, thunder and lightning break over the city; Qinyan dreams he is seized by the ruffian just as he steps aboard, and wakes in a cold sweat to hear the parrot under the eaves announce, "Yesterday's man is here again." Ziyu rises early through the last of the rain, packing Jiangsu and Huzhou silks, small treasures and a dozen taels of loose silver to thank Sulan — only to be waylaid by Li Yuanmao, barefoot and half-dressed, who hums and haws his way around to borrowing money, so that Ziyu reaches the boat only at noon. Sulan tries him first: Yunong is too ill to come. Ziyu takes it like cold water poured over him and can say only that good things are much thwarted. Then Sulan offers a substitute of similar face; Ziyu refuses gravely — Yiyuan once played that trick already, and he will not have a maid imitate the lady. Before he finishes, a figure like flowering jade steps out of the after-cabin: Qinyan himself, thinner than before, once a peerless bloom and now a fallen blossom that says nothing, pale as a chrysanthemum. The three weep in one knot, and then the truth of Wei Pincai's plotting comes out; Ziyu, ashen, swears to have done with him. Wine is set out and the cloud lifts by degrees: each finds the other a jade branch, an autumn moon in a jar of ice, a rose washed with dew, a peony veiled in mist, and both wish they had a spear to drive the red sun backward. A boat crosses their bow with shrill pipes and hurrying strings, three men stripped to the waist drinking mouth to mouth with young actors in their laps — Sulan identifies Pan Qiguan, who has been hounding Su Huifang, and Yu Mei of the Dengchun troupe on his knee. One stretch of water divides two kinds of pleasure: on this side a quiet sorrow too full for speech, on that a laughter like a torrent going down rapids. Sunset drives them back; Ziyu distributes his gifts, tips the servants, and parts from Qinyan at the Great East Gate, where Qinyan says that hereafter they will meet either at Xiangwan's or at Yiyuan. Ziyu reaches home by lamplight and answers Madam Yan's questions with whatever comes to hand.`,
  },
  23: {
    zh: `本回上下两截，各写一个"欲"字的丑处。上截属李元茂。他从梅子玉的账房借得八吊大钱，先在烟铺换了十张票子，装在槟榔口袋里挂在衣衿上，志得意满；去寻母舅孙嗣徽扑了空，与孙嗣元话不投机，便信步逛到东园，心里还惦记着从前那个弹琵琶的妇人。他戴上眼镜，弯腰探头，看中一个体肥脸粗、眼波流转的三十来岁妇人；门边两个闲汉——一个挽着揪儿把玩麻鹰，一个穿蓝布衫——你一言我一语，说包管替他守门，一推把他推进门去，反手扣上。屋里先有汉子索钱，元茂只有票子，递出一吊只当能找零，那人看也不看便塞进皮抽屉。草帘一掀，腥气扑鼻，正在情热之际，忽听街门大响，两个闲汉扭打进来，四个人八只手把他剥个精光，一哄而散。元茂上天无路，只得裹了草帘蹲在破屋里高喊小使王保；那孩子才十三四岁，早躲在树后啼哭。看热闹的挤满门口，有人笑道这是好嫖的报应。衣裳取来穿上，他还假作体面，回头恐吓要送兵马司。原来那妇人与两个闲汉本是一路，专候这样呆头呆脑而衣履光鲜的外行。此事他有冤无处诉，偏偏人丛中有认得他的，知他住在梅宅、又是孙亮功未过门的女婿；后来王保打破茶碗挨了两掌，索性把这段丑事说了出去，孙亮功也就风闻了。下截随孙嗣徽转出姬亮轩：江苏金匮人，久办刑钱，因品行不端，近省竟寻不出馆地，只得携资进京钻营。作者借此把作幕的分作数等：有科名不遂而改途、慈祥济世、正大光明的上幕，十中不过二三；有胁肩谄笑、以亲戚同乡结党、开口只论束修多寡、到馆则奉成案为圭臬的劣幕；更有《四书》只读得一半、拜师学幕而吃喝嫖赌招摇撞骗的下下之流——姬亮轩正是最末一等。是日嗣徽请他听凤台班的戏，又携伶人凤林入酒楼雅座。姬亮轩身边有个十五六岁的徽州少年巴英官，本是剃头铺学徒，十千钱买来改名装扮，日间主仆称呼，夜间妻妾侍奉；巴英官气忿站在廊下，凤林最伶俐，几声"巴二爷"便哄住了。席上一个满口之乎者也而不通，一个满口下作而自得：凤林说班中师傅一年可挣三四千吊，姬亮轩伸出舌头，说比刑钱束修还多，懊悔当初不曾学戏；又自夸这一个少年兼作"驴子与小妾"，月给八百钱，一年不到二十千，比叫相公便宜十倍；末了竟以坐船坐车作譬，说到粪车与木耳，秽亵不堪，连凤林也红了脸罚他三杯。回末作者自解：世间实有此等人，若只写美人名士的好色不淫，而不写几个极淫的，便不成五色之文、八音之律。`,
    en: `The chapter falls into two halves, each exhibiting a different ugliness of appetite. The first belongs to Li Yuanmao. Having got eight strings of cash out of the Mei counting-house through Mei Ziyu, he changes five of them into ten paper notes at a tobacconist's, stows them in the betel pouch hanging at his lapel, and swells with confidence. He calls on his uncle Sun Sihui and finds him out, sits briefly with Sun Siyuan, whose contradictions he cannot bear, and then drifts toward the East Garden quarter, still thinking of the woman with the pipa he once saw there. Spectacles on, stooping and craning, he settles on a fleshy, coarse-faced woman of thirty with roving, watery eyes. Two idlers at the door — one with his hair in a topknot, toying with a little hawk, the other in a blue cloth shirt — take turns coaxing him, promise to stand guard, then shove him through the doorway and bolt it behind him. A filthy man appears demanding payment; Yuanmao has only notes, hands over one worth a string expecting change, and watches it vanish into a leather drawer unexamined. Behind the straw curtain the air is rank; and just as his heart is hottest the street door crashes, the two idlers come brawling in, and four men with eight hands strip him bare and scatter. With no hole to hide in, Yuanmao winds the straw curtain round himself, crouches in the hovel and shouts for his page Wang Bao — a boy of thirteen or fourteen who had wandered off to play and is now weeping behind a tree. A crowd packs the doorway; someone laughs that this is what whoring earns. When his clothes are fetched he dresses, walks out with borrowed dignity, and threatens over his shoulder to have the culprits handed to the ward office. The woman and the two idlers were of course one gang, waiting precisely for a well-dressed simpleton with no experience. Yuanmao can complain to nobody, but people in the crowd know him for a resident of the Mei house and Sun Lianggong's betrothed son-in-law; later Wang Bao, slapped for breaking a teacup, tells the whole story, and word reaches Sun Lianggong himself. The second half follows Sun Sihui to his kinsman Ji Liangxuan, a man of Jinkui in Jiangsu, long employed as a legal-and-fiscal secretary, whose character is so bad that no post can be found for him in the nearer provinces; so he has come to the capital with money and goods to buy his way into notice. The narrator pauses to sort the secretarial profession into its kinds: at the top, scholars whose examination luck failed, who take up the work late and practise it uprightly, guarding both their employer's name and the people's welfare — two or three in ten at most; below them the mean sort, all bowed shoulders and ingratiating smiles, who form cliques of kinsmen, countrymen and old connections, sell recommendations, ask first how large the fee is, and once installed treat precedent as scripture and let hired copyists do the work; and lowest of all, men who have read half of *The Four Books*, apprentice themselves to a "teacher" for the price of one banquet, and then live by drinking, gambling, whoring, and swindling — Ji Liangxuan's own class exactly. That day Sihui takes him to the Fengtai troupe's performance and then to a private upstairs room with the actor Fenglin. Ji keeps a handsome page of fifteen or sixteen from Huizhou, Ba Yingguan, bought out of a barber's shop for ten thousand cash, renamed and dressed up: master and servant by day, husband and concubine by night. Yingguan sulks jealously in the corridor until the quick-witted Fenglin soothes him with a few cries of "Second Master Ba." At table one man talks unintelligible Classics and the other unblushing filth. Fenglin mentions that a troupe master clears three or four thousand strings a year; Ji puts out his tongue in astonishment — more than a secretary's fee — and says he regrets not having learned opera instead. He then boasts of his page as at once donkey and concubine: messenger by day, company by night, relief in emergencies, eight hundred cash a month and under twenty strings a year, a tenth of the cost of calling for actors. He ends with the notorious comparison of boats and carts, night-soil carts and wood-ear fungus, until even Fenglin reddens and fines him three cups. The author closes by defending the filth: such people do exist, and a book that shows only beauties and gentlemen who love beauty without lechery, and never the utterly lecherous, would be no pattern of five colours, no harmony of eight tones.`,
  },
  24: {
    zh: `颜仲清与王恂在家闲话，把连日新闻一件件抖开。先是张仲雨传来的：开银号的潘其观自三月起便想认苏蕙芳作干儿子，蕙芳佯作殷勤，先借他二百吊钱，待他要敬皮杯时假装鱼骨鲠喉，再把一干人灌得烂醉，安置同宿客房；潘其观滚下床来，尿在自己鞋里，醒后查问，只说蕙芳被华光宿叫去了，闹了一夜也只得回家；四月再来纠缠，又撞上假查夜的，吓得丢下一只金镯逃走。其次是李元茂东园裹草帘的丑事，已由张仲雨的人目睹传开。再次是魏聘才自进华府便变了相：闹春阳馆，累店家挨了二十嘴巴，又买通华府车夫与厨下小子去糟蹋杜琴言。末一件却是喜信——梅子玉与琴言、陆素兰同游运河一日，两人的病竟都好了。由新闻转入议论，才是本回正文。王恂不解：子玉与琴言不过正月初六看《惊梦》的一面之缘，何以缠绵至此？仲清答得极透：这一段至正之情的情根，偏由魏聘才这不正之人种下——当日同船北上，聘才细数琴言的好处，子玉听熟了便日日思想；及见其色艺冠群，又闻其孤高冷淡，爱中生敬，敬中愈生爱；再得徐子云作氤氲使，以假试真，才把两副肺腑打通。子玉一怒而琴言感愈深，琴言一哭而子玉爱弥甚，虽只一面，已如三生前定。论到田春航与蕙芳，则起于色而终于敬，正如子云、相如同工异曲；至于潘其观，仲清断言他要认干儿子恰是其无情处——若得银号而不遂欢心，必恨蕙芳入骨；情字若走到守钱虏心上，天上情关也须另请情仙执掌。仲清又逐一品题：徐子云爱博而不泛、气盛而不骄，是情中主人；萧次贤野鹤闲云而纯静过人；史南湘恃才傲物而用情恳挚；高品如云行水流，辞锋虽利，独于所好不加一刻薄语；刘文泽与王恂和平浑厚；自己则不肯轻用情，只把情借与人用，成人之情即以成己之情——王恂笑称这是朱家、郭解一流的"情中之侠"。至于魏聘才、冯子佩、潘其观是情中之蠹，奚十一以木桶暗害相公是情中之盗，孙嗣徽、孙嗣元则根本不知情为何物。正说得有味，李玉林同王桂保来了，带来怡园的消息：六月初六荷花生日，华光宿要来逛园，徐子云与萧次贤商定，从各班挑人合成一个大成班，另谱新戏。戏目取毛声山在《琵琶记》评语中拟而未作的《补天石》诸目——《李陵返汉》《燕丹灭秦》《诸葛延年》《明妃归汉》，次贤又添四本：《金谷园绿珠投楼》《马嵬驿杨妃随驾》《李谪仙夜郎奉诏》《杜拾遗金殿承恩》。仲清极称这四本为天下才子佳人吐气，并为杨妃辨诬：祸在国忠，何罪玉妃；洗儿之说新旧《唐书》皆不载，不过因元微之一句轻薄词句以讹传讹。旦脚十人是袁宝珠、苏蕙芳、陆素兰、王兰保、金漱芳、林春喜、李玉林、王桂保并杜琴言、玉艳，王恂笑称"十美班"。饭后行《贴翠令》，酒酣时王恂请二人品评同侪：宝珠不慕势利而意气自豪，蕙芳一尘不染而心怀好胜，素兰和而不流、婉而有致，兰保雅俗咸宜，漱芳恬静妥贴，春喜心灵口敏，桂保泛应有余、风流自赏，琴言别有会心、海枯石烂而节操不移；玉林自谦无可取，桂保便替他补上"芳洁自守，不亢不卑"。说到蓉官、二喜、玉美之流，桂保比作助纣为虐的旁门左道，道玉林之事是蓉官攻出来的，袁琪官之苦是二喜作成的——由此牵出奚十一以木桶暗算琪官、琪官性烈毁桶而出、双手刮得稀烂的惨事，也带出奚家由洋商而军功、坐拥千万而以鸦片致富的来历。桂保又替琴言担心：他见一个得罪一个，心上却只有一个梅子玉。二更方散。`,
    en: `Yan Zhongqing and Wang Xun sit at home and unpack the week's news item by item. First, from Zhang Zhongyu: Pan Qiguan, who keeps a silver bureau, has been trying since the third month to make Su Huifang his adopted son. Huifang played warm, borrowed two hundred strings of cash from him, and when Pan wanted a lip-to-lip toast feigned a fishbone in his throat; then he got the whole party blind drunk and bedded them down together in the guest room. Pan rolled off the bed, urinated into his own shoe, and on waking was told only that Huifang had been summoned by Hua Guangsu; he raged all night and went home with nothing. Returning in the fourth month, he ran into a faked night patrol and fled, dropping a gold bracelet. Next, Li Yuanmao's straw-curtain disgrace at the East Garden, witnessed by Zhang Zhongyu's man and now everywhere repeated. Next, Wei Pincai, who has changed beyond recognition since entering the Hua mansion: the uproar at the Chunyang Restaurant that cost the manager twenty blows on the face, and the bribing of a Hua carriage driver and a kitchen boy to abuse Du Qinyan. Last, a piece of good news — Mei Ziyu spent a day on the canal with Qinyan and Lu Sulan, and both invalids have recovered. From gossip the chapter turns to its real subject, an anatomy of feeling. Wang Xun cannot understand it: Ziyu and Qinyan met only once, on the sixth of the first month, over a performance of *Waking from a Dream*. Zhongqing's answer is exact. The root of this most upright love was planted by a most unupright man — Wei Pincai, who came north on the same boat and catalogued Qinyan's excellences until Ziyu thought of him daily before ever seeing his face. Then came the sight of his unrivalled art and the report of his proud, cold nature, so that love bred reverence and reverence bred more love; then Xu Ziyun played matchmaker, testing the false against the true, and opened one heart into the other. Ziyu's anger deepened Qinyan's gratitude, Qinyan's tears deepened Ziyu's love, and a single meeting became a bond fixed three lives ago. Tian Chunhang's love for Huifang, by contrast, began in desire and ended in reverence — the same craft in a different key. As for Pan Qiguan, Zhongqing insists that wanting Huifang for an adopted son is the very proof of his heartlessness: had he spent his silver and been refused his pleasure, he would have hated Huifang to the bone. If feeling ever lodged in a miser's breast, heaven's gate of passion would need a new keeper. Zhongqing then appraises them all: Xu Ziyun loves widely without diffuseness and is spirited without arrogance — the master of feeling; Xiao Cixian is a wild crane among idle clouds, unmatched in purity; Shi Nanxiang is proud of his gifts yet wholly sincere in affection; Gao Pin moves like cloud and water and, sharp-tongued as he is, will not say one cruel word of anyone he loves; Liu Wenze and Wang Xun are peaceable and generous. He himself will not spend feeling lightly, but lends it to others, completing their loves and so completing his own — at which Wang Xun laughs that he is a knight-errant of feeling in the line of Zhu Jia and Guo Jie. Wei Pincai, Feng Zipei, and Pan Qiguan are the worms of feeling; Xi Shiyi, who traps young actors in a wooden barrel, is its bandit; Sun Sihui and Sun Siyuan simply do not know what feeling is. Li Yulin and Wang Guibao arrive with news from Yiyuan. On the sixth of the sixth month, the lotus birthday, Hua Guangsu is to visit the garden, and Xu Ziyun has agreed with Xiao Cixian to pick the best of every troupe into one grand company and stage newly written plays. The titles come from *Stone to Mend Heaven*, the set Mao Shengshan proposed in his commentary on *Story of the Pipa* but never composed — *Li Ling Returns to Han*, *Prince Dan Destroys Qin*, *Zhuge Yan Nian*, *Bright Consort Returns to Han* — to which Cixian has added four more: *Green Pearl Throws Herself from the Golden Valley Garden*, *Consort Yang Follows the Carriage at Mawei Post*, *Li the Banished Immortal Receives the Edict at Yelang*, and *Du the Remonstrator Honored in the Golden Hall*. Zhongqing praises these last as vindication for every gifted man and beautiful woman, and defends Consort Yang in particular: the catastrophe was Yang Guozhong's, not hers, and the tale of the bathing of the infant appears in neither the old nor the new *Book of Tang*, having grown out of one frivolous line by Yuan Weizhi. The ten female leads are Yuan Baozhu, Su Huifang, Lu Sulan, Wang Lanbao, Jin Shufang, Lin Chunxi, Li Yulin, Wang Guibao, Du Qinyan, and Yuyan — a "Troupe of Ten Beauties," Wang Xun says. After dinner they play the *Affixing the Kingfisher Command*, and over wine Wang Xun asks the two actors to appraise their colleagues: Baozhu scorns influence yet carries himself proudly; Huifang is spotless, luminous, and privately competitive; Sulan is harmonious without drifting, gentle without weakness; Lanbao suits high company and low alike; Shufang is calm and apt in speech; Chunxi is quick of mind and tongue; Guibao meets all comers and admires his own dash; Qinyan understands what others cannot construe and would keep his integrity though seas dried and stones crumbled. Yulin claims nothing for himself, so Guibao supplies it: fragrant and self-contained, neither haughty nor servile. Of Rongguan, Er Xi, Yu Mei and their sort, Guibao says they belong to the heterodox school that abets tyranny — it was Rongguan who exposed Yulin, and Er Xi who engineered Yuan Qiguan's suffering. That leads to the cruelty itself: Xi Shiyi's barrel trap, and Yuan Qiguan, too fierce to submit, breaking out of it with both hands flayed raw. With it comes Xi's pedigree — a foreign-trade fortune turned into military rank, ten million in property, opium money underneath it all. Guibao ends by worrying over Qinyan, who offends everyone he meets and keeps room in his heart for one man only, Mei Ziyu. They break up at the second watch.`,
  },
  25: {
    zh: `六月初六荷花生日，怡园为华光宿设宴演新戏。刘文泽因其父刘侍郎升了礼部尚书，星夜自保定赶回，是日辰刻先到。书中于此细写怡园：周围三四里，小山堆以太湖石，大山以土为脚，护以花木，衬以亭台；山洞暗设桔槔戽水倒喷，便成飞瀑；池通外河，回环随势；园内另设射圃、球场、渔庄、稻舍、酒肆、茶寮，皆园丁开设，使执事人不必出外旷业，这是徐子云的用心。庭院二十四处，认真游赏须八九日方尽。正中含万楼取"含万物而化光"之意，东边垂杨之外便是吟秋水榭，荷花开满一池。子云向文泽说明今日客少之故：梅子玉旧病复发，史南湘醉后呕血，田春航因未会过华公子不肯来，王恂在家守着夫人临盆，颜仲清见众人皆辞也辞了，高品尚未回京，只剩张仲雨与萧次贤。仲雨新捐了六品，总共花去四千，还是起盛银号的潘其观替他垫了五百；文泽笑他将来必名利双收，子云接口道"上司一定欢喜，只是百姓吃苦些"，仲雨也不讳言，说自己这官原是花本钱来的。华公子乘椅轿而至，随从二十余人；两家父辈本是军营旧交，子云长他五岁，遂以兄事之。席上十旦捧牙笏参见，华公子独于杜琴言留意，说他"举止似乎未熟，然而秀外慧中，觉有出尘之致"。开场《李陵返汉》《明妃入关》，继以《仪郎奉诏》，把李谪仙的魂魄都做了出来；第二本《杨妃入蜀》则国忠伏诛、六军踊跃，明皇自吹玉笛，妃子歌《清平》，宫人作《回风》之舞，八位女仙自扎成的峨嵋山顶御风而下，百和龙涎香烟缭绕，竟把一座戏台放在彩云端里。华公子喝采不绝，赏钱八十吊，又命林珊枝捧出一盘梅花、如意、菱角式的金锞分赏十旦；自家的八龄班也上来唱了《群仙高会》。未末移席吟秋水榭，十旦扮采莲女荡舟花间，唱《采莲歌》，声如子夜清歌，望如湘君游戏。华公子兴犹未尽，要"重歌《金缕》，再舞《霓裳》"，子云说苏蕙芳与陆素兰的《独占》、袁宝珠与琴言的《惊梦》《寻梦》皆绝妙无双，可惜雷同；文泽便提议二人同唱，遂有琴言自东、宝珠自西同演《寻梦》一段奇景：一个如华月初升、春云冉冉，一个如娇花欲放、晓露犹含，两声相和，如碧树间么凤雏凰。华公子拍案叫绝，说从此才信人间真有绝色，悔从前把嫫母无盐也置之金屋；又评二人"五雀六燕，轻重适均，离之则独绝，合之则两全"。宝珠为公子赏识而益加精神，琴言心里却不愿，只因惹不起，又见华公子眉目略似子玉，索性把他权当作子玉在楼上，照正月那日的情景重演一回。林珊枝眼看要关城，又暗含醋意，几番以目示意，子云会意劝驾。华公子临行执琴言之手，赞他"可夸京城独步"，解下汉玉双龙佩相赠，又请子云许可，另以一佩赏宝珠。客散后子云命众人换了便衣，廊前四盏素灯下重开一席。众人皆劝琴言领情：文泽说星北之倾倒不下于子玉，次贤说外间传闻不可信，不宜太冷淡。琴言越想越气——度香明知他与子玉相好，竟也说这等话——竟淌下泪来。张仲雨醉后冷笑，说难道赞也赞坏了，人家费尽心力还巴结不到这一赞；琴言忍不住顶了回去：我不会巴结人，也不像那自己巴结了还要教人巴结的。一句话说得仲雨变色，只碍着子云暂忍。子云托词他醉了，命宝珠携他入内；宝珠取出两块玉佩比了比，自己的略小一些。这一句硬话，日后却生出无数苦况来。`,
    en: `On the sixth of the sixth month, the lotus's birthday, Yiyuan entertains Hua Guangsu with the new plays. Liu Wenze, back from Baoding by forced stages since his father Vice-Minister Liu rose to Minister of Rites, arrives first, in the early cool. Here the garden is set out in full: three or four li round, its small hills piled of Taihu stone and its great hills earth-footed and crowned with peaks, screened with flowering trees and set off by pavilions; in a grotto a hidden well-sweep throws water upward into a flying cascade; the pond draws from the outer river and winds wide or narrow as the ground allows. Within are an archery ground, a ball court, a fishing hamlet, rice cottages, a wine shop and a tea booth, all kept by the gardeners so that the staff need never idle away outside — Xu Ziyun's own idea. Of the twenty-four courtyards, a serious visitor would need eight or nine days to see them all. At the centre stands the Hanwan Tower, named for containing all things and transmuting them into light; east of the weeping willows lies the Yinqiu Water Pavilion, its pond one sheet of lotus. Ziyun explains why the company is thin: Mei Ziyu has relapsed, Shi Nanxiang has vomited blood after drinking, Tian Chunhang will not come because he has never met Hua, Wang Xun is at home waiting on his wife's confinement, Yan Zhongqing withdrew when the rest did, and Gao Pin is not yet back — leaving only Zhang Zhongyu and Xiao Cixian. Zhongyu appears in the sixth grade, his rank freshly bought at a total of four thousand, five hundred of it advanced by Pan Qiguan of the Qisheng silver bureau. Wenze predicts he will reap both fame and profit; Ziyun adds that his superiors will certainly be pleased and only the people will suffer; Zhongyu cheerfully agrees, since an office bought with capital must be made to pay. Hua arrives in a chair with twenty and more attendants — the two fathers were comrades in the same campaign, and Ziyun, five years the elder, is treated as a brother. Ten young leads present themselves with ivory tablets, and of them all Hua fixes on Du Qinyan, remarking that his bearing is not yet practised but that he is elegant without and clever within, with an air from beyond the dust. The programme opens with *Li Ling Returns to Han* and *Bright Consort Enters the Pass*, then *The Envoy Receives the Edict*, which conjures up the very soul of Li the Banished Immortal. The second piece, *Consort Yang Enters Shu*, executes Yang Guozhong, rouses the six armies, and carries the emperor to Mount Emei, where he plays the jade flute himself while the consort sings *Clear Peace* and a palace lady dances the *Whirling Wind*; then eight female immortals ride the wind down from a scaffolded Emei wreathed in ambergris smoke, until the whole stage seems lodged in coloured cloud. Hua's applause never stops. He rewards eighty strings of cash and has Lin Shanzhi bring out a tray of gold ingots shaped like plum blossoms, ruyi sceptres and water-caltrops to divide among the ten; his own boys' troupe then comes up to perform *Immortals' Grand Assembly*. In the late afternoon the party moves to the Yinqiu Water Pavilion, where the ten, dressed as lotus-gatherers, pole among the flowers singing the *Lotus-Picking Song*, a sound like midnight airs, a sight like the Lady of the Xiang at play. Still unsatisfied, Hua calls for the *Golden Thread* to be sung again and the *Nishang* danced again, so that each may show his art. Ziyun observes that the incomparable pieces are Su Huifang and Lu Sulan's *Exclusive Possession* and Yuan Baozhu and Qinyan's *Waking from a Dream* and *Pursuing the Dream* — a pity they overlap. Wenze proposes the two sing together, and so comes the marvel of the doubled *Pursuing the Dream*: Qinyan entering from the east like a bright moon newly risen, his steps drifting spring cloud; Baozhu from the west like a flower on the point of opening with dawn dew still in it, his steps slender willows swaying; the two voices answering like young phoenixes among jade trees. Hua strikes the table and cries that only now does he believe in perfect beauty, repenting that he ever housed plain women in embroidered rooms; and he judges them evenly matched, each unrivalled alone and each completed by the other. Baozhu, warmed by such notice, performs the better for it; Qinyan is unwilling but dares not offend, and, seeing something of Ziyu in Hua's face, resolves to pretend that Ziyu himself sits in the gallery and to sing as he sang on that day in the first month. Lin Shanzhi, watching the gates and privately jealous, signals until Ziyun takes the hint and speeds his guest. At parting Hua takes Qinyan's hand, calls his singing unmatched in the capital, and tears off a Han-jade double-dragon pendant for him, then asks Ziyun's leave to give another to Baozhu. When the guests have gone the company changes into plain clothes and sits again under four glass lamps on the verandah. All of them press Qinyan to feel honoured: Wenze says Xingbei's admiration is no less than Ziyu's, Cixian that the rumours about Hua are unreliable and Qinyan should not be so cold. Qinyan grows angrier the longer he thinks — that Xu Ziyun, of all people, knowing what binds him to Ziyu, should talk so. His tears fall. Zhang Zhongyu, drunk, sneers that praise can hardly spoil a man and that others toil for years without earning such praise; and Qinyan answers that he does not want praise and cannot fawn, being unlike those who fawn themselves and then teach others to fawn. Zhongyu changes colour and swallows it only out of deference to his host. Ziyun covers the moment by calling Qinyan drunk and sending Baozhu in with him; inside, Baozhu holds the two pendants side by side and finds his own the smaller. That one hard sentence will breed sorrows without number.`,
  },
  26: {
    zh: `华光宿掌灯后才回府，向苏浣香略说了怡园光景，便到星栊卧室来。十个"珠"字婢一色白罗衫、麻姑髻、素馨花，因随主母到家庙进香才改的装：明珠捧上冰梅汤，掌珠拿了鹅毛扇轻轻打着，荷珠替他换了轻纱衫。他细开一张消暑粥的方子——盛暑是发散时候，防风露、薄荷露少用，玫瑰、香稻、荷花、桂花诸露多加，茯苓、莲子、琼糜、燕窝诸粉各依分两，文武火慢熬，一时二刻不得见铜器，还须亲手做，不许经老婆子的手。廊下铜炉银吊慢熬着粥，婢子们笑闹推挤，说到秋天"收房"的话，滚作一团。爱珠提绛纱灯去传林珊枝。公子要遣人请魏聘才到留青精舍说话，珊枝知道不过是看戏闲谈，便以园门已锁、开三重门须到二更为辞挡了回去。公子却问起：那些相公为何总不进府？珊枝答，朔望原是清早就来的，门上只说爷未起身便挡了驾，班子里请安也不挂号簿。又问琴言是谁的徒弟，答是曹长庆的——正是珊枝旧日的师傅，当年因性气不合，被长庆挑换了登春班的绣芳，绣芳出师得了八千吊，长庆才买下琴言。公子再问那出《寻梦》谁强，珊枝答宝珠唱得熟，琴言初次尚生，第二次略好；公子却说论相貌还算琴言，身上像有仙骨。掌珠只顾听着，一扇子脱手落地，明珠嗤笑，公子便说改日叫那班人进来唱一天，还要十婢扮生与他们合串。次日公子直睡到巳正才起。他在华夫人房中用饭，说八龄班万不及人家一分，又说夫人的相貌像苏蕙芳，夫人听了不悦。书中于此点明：这一对夫妻本是琴瑟相和，公子好奢而不淫；夫人几次要替他收房，他反说一作了妾便无趣，不如伺候几年配个青年美貌的，是件功德；又说夫人如牡丹，杂花种到牡丹台上，不是效颦邻女，便是婢学夫人——所以任他繁华，夫人绝不生疑。这一顿饭直谈到午后，公子又在夫人床上睡着，竟把魏聘才忘在留青精舍。聘才奉召之喜如金殿传胪，一路对林珊枝老珊长、老弟短，进门只顾细看那镂空嵌宝的窗棂屏门，却足足饿等了两个时辰，茶把肠子洗得精空，肚里咕噜如饿鸱叫；珊枝在洗红轩外等得也饿，还是明珠让了些残馔，爱珠故意刁难不肯通传，末了荷珠进去回了。公子出来赔了不是，命捧上八样点心，一面说自己费心买的八个伶人本想压倒外边，如今反被外边压倒。聘才立刻迎合：好花不在多，拣顶好的买几个便是；心里却另有一本账——琴言心上只有梅子玉，一旦进了府，此生休想再见；他那古怪脾气，长久必被厌弃，正好弄他进来受两年苦，以出胸中恶气。公子碍着徐子云，说这两个都是度香所爱；聘才便说度香真爱的是袁宝珠，琴言不过是幌子，他师傅长庆最爱钱，一说必肯。公子终被说动，只叮嘱"可行则行"，又不甘落在度香之后。次日聘才带了四儿出城，邀上叶茂林同去，路上遇见张仲雨，附耳说了许多，聘才只点头道"进来了还由得他"。到长庆家，长庆先淡淡相待，一听是华府师爷便满面添花，开出烟灯请他躺下，夸琴言一面之赏便得十个金锞、并公子的玉佩，值七百吊、二百吊不等。及至说破来意，长庆便哭穷：孩子去年十月才买来，一教就会，才十五岁，出师还有五年，一家二十余口全靠他一人。叶茂林劝以华府之势与日后执事顶戴，聘才劝以少年发身难保，转眼便是白丢；长庆只推要问琴言愿否。聘才索性揭他的短：除了徐子云谁喜欢他？他为梅少爷病成那样，人人厌他，若指望靠梅少爷发迹，除非公鸡生蛋。一句正戳中长庆的心病。及至开价，长庆算准华公子是出名的冤大头，暗想五六千两总讲得出口；聘才摇头道男孩子哪值上万，四五千已尽。长庆摇头装哭，聘才便勃然作色，说等你去发大财，过几天自己送上门来时便一钱不值，拂袖上车，只低声约叶茂林在六合馆相候。长庆终舍不得这棵摇钱树，说要问过徒弟，两日后再回话。聘才无可奈何，只得回府托林珊枝转禀，说没有找着长庆，改日再去。`,
    en: `Hua Guangsu reaches home well after lamp-lighting, gives Su Huanxiang a brief account of Yiyuan, and goes through to the Star-Latticed Chamber. The ten pearl-named maids stand in matching white silk, hair in Magu knots with a ball of jasmine at the front, having changed dress to follow their mistress to the family shrine. Mingzhu brings iced plum broth, Zhangzhu plies a goose-feather fan, Hezhu changes him into gauze. He dictates a recipe for a cooling porridge with fastidious precision — high summer being a dispersing season, less mint and fangfeng dew, more rose, fragrant-rice, lotus and cassia; poria, lotus-seed and bird's-nest powders by weight; slow fire; no bronze vessel may touch it for an hour and a quarter; and it must be made by her own hands, not passed through the old women's. Under the eaves a silver pot simmers over a brazier while the maids tease and tussle over which of them will be taken into the bedchamber come autumn. Aizhu carries a crimson lantern to summon Lin Shanzhi. Hua wants Wei Pincai fetched to the Liuqing Study; Shanzhi, guessing it is only theatre gossip, objects that the gates are locked and three of them would have to be opened, and the errand is dropped. Hua asks instead why the actors never come to his mansion. On the first and fifteenth they do come early, says Shanzhi, but the gatemen turn them away saying the master has not risen, and troupe visits are never entered in the register. Whose disciple is Qinyan? Cao Changqing's — Shanzhi's own former master, who once traded him to the Dengchun troupe for Xiufang after a quarrel, and who bought Qinyan only after Xiufang graduated for eight thousand strings. Which of the two was better in *Pursuing the Dream*? Yuan Baozhu sings with more practice, Shanzhi answers; Qinyan was raw the first time and better the second. But in looks, says Hua, Qinyan has the advantage — the boy seems to have immortal bones in him. Zhangzhu, listening too hard, lets the fan slip out of her hand; Mingzhu snorts; and Hua promises to have the troupe in one day and make his ten maids play the male roles opposite them. He sleeps late, and takes breakfast in Madam Hua's room, telling her that his own boys' troupe is not worth a tenth of theirs, and that she resembles Su Huifang — which she does not enjoy hearing. Here the novel pauses on the marriage: the two are genuinely devoted, and Hua, extravagant as he is, is not lecherous. Several times his wife has offered to let him take a maid as concubine, and he has refused, saying a maid once made concubine loses all her charm, and it is a better deed to keep them a few years and then marry them to handsome young men; besides, were his wife plain he might look elsewhere, but as she is, any concubine set beside her would be a common flower planted on a peony terrace. So however lavish he grows, she never suspects him. The meal runs past noon, Hua falls asleep on her bed, and Wei Pincai is left forgotten in the Liuqing Study. Summoned to breakfast, Pincai had been as elated as a man called up in the Golden Hall, flattering Lin Shanzhi the whole way; now he sits two full hours among the openwork carved windows and jewel-inlaid screens, tea washing his stomach empty until it growls like a hungry owl. Shanzhi, waiting outside the Xihong Pavilion, is fed leftovers by Mingzhu, while Aizhu refuses to carry word in and Hezhu at last does. Hua comes out apologizing, orders eight kinds of pastry, and confesses that the eight singers he bought to crush all rivals have themselves been crushed. Pincai instantly agrees that fine flowers need not be many, only the finest — while privately reckoning that Qinyan's heart holds nothing but Mei Ziyu, that once inside these walls he will never see him again, that his strange temper must in time disgust his new owner, and that two years of such misery would settle the grudge nicely. Hua objects that both boys are Xu Ziyun's favourites; Pincai answers that Ziyun's true favourite is Yuan Baozhu, that Qinyan is only a screen, and that his master Changqing loves money above everything and will jump at the offer. Hua yields, saying only that it should be tried and dropped if refused — and grumbling that even to buy him now is to come a move behind Duxiang. Next morning Pincai takes Si'er out of the city, collects Ye Maolin, and meets Zhang Zhongyu on the road, who whispers a great deal in his ear; Pincai only nods and says that once the boy is inside, the matter will hardly rest with him. Changqing receives them coolly until he hears that this is the Hua mansion's Master Wei, whereupon he blossoms, opens the opium lamp, and boasts that a single meeting earned Qinyan ten gold ingots and the young lord's own jade pendant, worth seven hundred strings and two hundred more. When the purpose is named he pleads poverty: the boy was bought only last October, learns everything at a touch, is barely fifteen with five years to graduation, and twenty-odd mouths depend on him. Ye Maolin urges the weight of the Hua name and the prospect of a post and a button; Pincai urges that boys of fifteen may spoil within a year and leave the investment worthless. Changqing keeps saying he must ask the boy. So Pincai strikes at the sore place: apart from Xu Ziyun, who likes Qinyan? He fell ill for Young Master Mei, everyone is sick of him, and if he means to rise through Young Master Mei, roosters will lay eggs. Changqing, whose complaints of temper were only bargaining, has nothing to answer. At the price he reckons Hua a famous soft touch and hints at five or six thousand taels; Pincai shakes his head — no boy is worth ten thousand, four or five is the limit. Changqing shakes his head and pretends to weep; Pincai flares up, tells him to go and get rich and warns that in a few days he will bring the boy to the door himself and find him worth nothing, then sweeps out to his carriage, murmuring to Ye Maolin to meet him at the Liuhe Restaurant. Changqing, unable to part with his money tree, will only promise an answer in two days. Pincai, out of moves, goes back and has Lin Shanzhi report that Changqing could not be found and that he will call again another day.`,
  },
  27: {
    zh: `魏聘才碰了软钉子回来，盘算再唆使那车夫、厨子上门吵闹，只须隔几天下手，免得看出破绽。笔锋随即转到奚十一：他在长芦盐务里躲了一月，恰逢洋船到岸，汇了两万银子并五千货物，重新阔起来；木桶事已冷，便回京照旧胡闹。他官名奚正绅，因岭南口音"十一"近"土"，人又叫他奚老土。相与最密的是张仲雨与潘其观：仲雨在帐局子里迎逢，早探明奚家底细，及奚十一告贷时替他向潘其观银号借了一万，九扣之外又私扣一千，奚十一到手只八千。作者比较二恶：潘其观虽丑，却胆小、惧内、又怕被讹，心淫而胆怯；奚十一恃财恃势，无法无天，人号"烟熏太岁"，且自忖在京不过一年半载，不闹个淋漓尽致便不像个公子。他新聘的刑钱朋友，正是第二十三回那位坐粪车的姬亮轩，由孙亮功转托王文辉荐来，年修一千二百两；姬亮轩几次把巴英官送与东家，英官伺候过大老爷，反在师爷面前骄纵起来。一日张仲雨在奚寓早饭，蓉官、春林侑酒，奚十一一饭之间两次入内，众人挤眉弄眼。蓉官问起一个姓魏的如今何等体面，仲雨说他在华公府当师爷发了财；姬亮轩问华府是否除皇帝就数他家，仲雨说单庄头就有四十几个，一年租课抵得一府钱漕。谈锋一转说到秋水堂的杜琴言，蓉官、春林齐说他架子大：终席不言，不斟酒，不请安，赏他东西也不谢，连袁宝珠、苏蕙芳都没有这样。奚十一夸口必要一试，仲雨冷笑道：别说你奚老土，就是徐子云、华光宿两位大公子，也常受他的气，你连他的屋都进不去。这边琴言自怡园回来日日啼哭，一恐将来进府应酬不来，二恨每逢热闹独不见梅子玉；曹长庆进来说破聘才买人之意，琴言放声大哭，说师傅若不要他，他也不到别处去，只求一死了事。长庆出门前请了陆素兰来陪他。素兰这一番剖白，是全书写"情"最透彻的一段：他先说华公子未见得坏，进去混几年未必无益，只苦在见不着子玉；继而分作四层——子玉年少，事发必受家教之责，怨且归你；你心里有他，脸上却不可教人看破；你屡屡冲撞徐子云，久而久之他必心冷，那时师傅要在你身上讨还，比进华府更苦；到那步田地，子玉纵有泪也无力，你身在外如驴儿推磨，心在内如道士炼丹，才真要死了。琴言答以"重情而略事，行吾心之所安"，说到极处不过捐生以报；素兰便正色驳他：你死了不算忠臣烈士节妇义夫，明白人说你情痴，糊涂人说你呆子；子玉上有父母、下有功名子嗣，痛过之后终究要说一句镜花水月到眼皆空，只留你在阴司孤孤单单，那时还唱得成《阳告》么？一席话说得琴言破涕而笑。琴言又抱怨袁宝珠：若正月初六那出《惊梦》是他去唱，自己便不上台；前日的《寻梦》又偏要合演，他不肯让，自己也不敢输，才招出这场祸来。正吃着对门王兰保送来的西瓜，前面已闹翻了天。原来奚十一带着姬亮轩、春兰、巴英官坐车来访，被回说不在家，先怀三分怒；长庆权衡再三，不肯拿琴言应酬，却把大帽子压他，只说华公子已把人叫进府唱戏，又殷勤替他脱衫、开烟灯，才把火气按下；随后竟叫回天福、天寿两个师弟去陪烟。春兰、巴英官看不入眼，四处闲逛，从半掩的小门进去，隔着绿纱窗缝，看见床上坐着两个绝色的人。回去一说，奚十一如火烧心，掀翻众人直闯进内，撞倒长庆，踢开锁着的房门，扯下帐子、掀翻桌子，又揪住长庆连打五个嘴巴，扬言明日再来搜。琴言与素兰早从后门逃到王兰保家。事后众人聚议：聘才之怨未解，又添一个奚十一。王兰保献出一条妙计——明日就送琴言进华府，一文身价不要，只说孩子不懂规矩，权且进去伺候两月，好不好再说；将来有事仍可告假；华公子见他不能在外唱戏，必有厚赏，那时师傅有财有势，闲人再不敢上门；若先争身价，人便死死活活都是府里的了。素兰拍手叫妙，长庆心服，琴言至此亦无可奈何。当晚琴言移住素兰家；次日奚十一果然又来，遍搜不见，恶狠狠而去。`,
    en: `Rebuffed at Changqing's, Wei Pincai goes home calculating that another visit from the carriage driver and the kitchen boy, delayed a few days so the hand behind it will not show, should settle the matter. The narrative then turns to Xi Shiyi in person. A month in hiding at the Changlu salt administration ended when a fleet of foreign ships came in; his family being foreign traders, he remitted twenty thousand taels and five thousand in goods and is rich again, and with the barrel scandal grown cold he returns to the capital and his old habits. His formal name is Xi Zhengshen, but since in his southern speech "the Eleventh" sounds like the word for earth, people call him Xi the Earth. His two intimates are Zhang Zhongyu and Pan Qiguan. Zhongyu, who haunts counting-houses, had taken his measure early, and when Xi ran short arranged a loan of ten thousand from Pan Qiguan's bureau — discounted a tenth, with another thousand skimmed off for himself, so that Xi received eight. The narrator weighs the two villains: Pan, repulsive as he is, is timid, hen-pecked, and afraid of being fleeced, so his lechery is all appetite and no daring; Xi, armoured in money and rank, is lawless, nicknamed the Smoke-Blackened Grand Duke, and reasons that since his stay in the capital will be short, anything less than utter debauchery would make him look like no gentleman at all. His newly engaged legal secretary is none other than the night-soil-cart philosopher of chapter twenty-three, Ji Liangxuan, recommended through Sun Lianggong and Wang Wenhui at twelve hundred taels a year; Ji has lent his page Ba Yingguan to his employer more than once, and the boy, having served the master, now gives himself airs before the secretary. One morning Zhang Zhongyu breakfasts there with Rongguan and Chunlin to pour wine, while Xi twice retires within during a single meal and the company exchange glances. Rongguan asks about a certain Wei now grown so magnificent; Zhongyu explains that he is secretary in the Hua mansion and has made money. Ji asks whether the Hua house really ranks next after the emperor; Zhongyu answers that its forty-odd estate stewards bring in more rent in a year than a whole prefecture's tax. The talk turns to Du Qinyan at Autumn Water Hall, and both actors complain of his airs: silent through a whole banquet, never pouring wine, never bowing, not even thanking a giver — not Yuan Baozhu nor Su Huifang is half so proud. Xi boasts he will try him, and Zhongyu answers coldly that even Xu Ziyun and Hua Guangsu, great gentlemen both, put up with the boy's temper, and that Xi would not get inside his door. Meanwhile Qinyan has wept every day since Yiyuan, dreading the summons into the mansion and grieving that Mei Ziyu is never at any of the gatherings. When Cao Changqing comes in and explains Pincai's offer, he cries aloud that if his master no longer wants him he will not go elsewhere but simply cease to be. Changqing goes out, sending for Lu Sulan to sit with him. Sulan's argument is the book's clearest reasoning on feeling. Hua, he says, is not obviously bad, and years there might do no harm but for the loss of Ziyu; then he lays out four consequences — that Ziyu is young, and if the matter comes out he will be disciplined and Qinyan blamed; that Qinyan may keep his heart for Ziyu but must not let his face declare it; that his repeated coldness will in time chill Xu Ziyun, whose support alone keeps his master from squeezing him, and that the squeezing would be worse than the Hua mansion; and that when it came to that, Ziyu could offer nothing but tears, leaving Qinyan's body outside like a donkey at the mill and his heart within like a Daoist over his crucible. Qinyan replies that he weighs present feeling above future event, acts as his heart finds peace, and at the last will give his life to complete it — whereupon Sulan turns grave. Dying would make him no loyal minister, no martyr, no chaste wife; the clear-sighted would call him a love-fool and the muddled a simpleton; and Ziyu, who has parents to repay, a career to make, and no heir yet, would grieve and then conclude that all of it was flowers in a mirror and moonlight on water, leaving Qinyan alone in the shades with no one to hear him — could he sing *The Piteous Appeal* down there? At this Qinyan laughs through his tears. He also blames Yuan Baozhu: had Baozhu sung *Waking from a Dream* that sixth day of the first month, he would never have gone on stage at all; and the recent disaster came of the doubled *Pursuing the Dream*, where neither would yield and so he was forced to excel. They are eating watermelons sent over by Wang Lanbao from across the lane when uproar breaks out in front. Xi Shiyi has arrived with Ji Liangxuan, Chunlan, and Ba Yingguan, been told the boy is out, and taken offence. Changqing, unwilling to offer Qinyan, plays the great card instead — the young lord has already taken the troupe into the Hua mansion to sing — and pacifies him with the opium lamp and his own hands at his gown, then produces his other two disciples, Tianfu and Tianshou, to attend him. Chunlan and Yingguan, disgusted by what follows, wander into the inner court, push open a half-shut gate, and through a crack in the green gauze see two exquisite figures seated on a bed. Their report sets Xi ablaze. He throws off the boys who try to hold him, charges inward, knocks Changqing sprawling, kicks in the locked door, tears down the bed curtains and overturns the table, then seizes Changqing and slaps him five times, swearing to come back and search the place tomorrow. Qinyan and Sulan have already fled by the back gate to Wang Lanbao's. In council afterwards, with Pincai's grudge unsettled and Xi Shiyi now added, Wang Lanbao proposes the one workable plan: deliver Qinyan to the Hua mansion tomorrow, refuse every penny of purchase money, and say only that the boy does not know the rules and may serve two months on trial; leave can still be asked for him afterwards, and Hua, seeing that he can no longer sing outside, will certainly be generous, so that Changqing will have both money and protection and no ruffian will dare come to the door — whereas to bargain a price is to sell him body and soul. Sulan claps his hands, Changqing is convinced, and Qinyan, after this day, has nothing left to say. That night he moves to Sulan's house; the next day Xi Shiyi comes back as promised, ransacks the place, finds nothing, and departs snarling.`,
  },
  28: {
    zh: `曹长庆挨了打，只得同叶茂林到华府向魏聘才负荆请罪，愿即日送人，分文身价不要。聘才自以为前番发怒果然奏效，托林珊枝转禀。华光宿问何以不要身价，聘才答说他怕孩子不懂规矩，日后有错还可领回；华公子点头应允，却声明既进了府，无论领价与否，外面一概不准陪酒唱戏，又命账房每月给长庆二百两。杜琴言至此自知避无可避。他想见梅子玉一面，又怕相见徒增断肠、耳目又多；欲写信，则方寸已乱，万语千言无从下笔，末了取出平日拭泪的那方罗帕，另包四味药裹在里面，托陆素兰面交。次日入府，华公子细细打量，十分满意，即拨在留青精舍；又领他到苏浣香处叩见，夫人见他弱质婷婷、毫无优伶习气，也说了个"好"字。琴言暗中洒泪，过了几日见公子行事尚正经，才略觉放心；独对魏聘才只作息夫人不言之状。子玉一日到素兰处，遇见王兰保与袁琪官在座。素兰拿出那个无字纸包，帕中滚出四味药来，子玉先疑是诀别之物，几乎哭倒；素兰忙说玉侬无恙，把聘才买人、奚十一大闹、众人劝驾、连夜避祸、送进华府的始末细细说了。四味药是牵牛、独活、芍药、防己，还是琪官的跟人认得——他曾在药铺当过伙计。素兰解"独活"为离你而独居，兰保解"防己"为自守其身，子玉却把整个哑谜读通了：芍药一名将离，是说已经离了；既离便只有独活；独活于高墙之内，自然刻刻小心、守身如玉；至于牵牛，别无他解，必是约他七月七日牛女相会之期见一面。素兰拍手称是，约他七夕来住一天。琪官初见子玉，慕其丰标，因说魏聘才当日在船上如何下作，又咬牙骂起奚十一来。子玉回家便觉四肢不舒，次日竟大病：语言无次，一日数哭，梦中或唤玉侬，或唤香畹，或痛骂奚十一、魏聘才。颜夫人只当他中了邪，延医求签许愿，十余日毫无起色；颜仲清、王恂常来探视，心里明白却无法可施。夫人疑"玉侬"是个女子，唤云儿细问，云儿不肯招，直到取了板子才跪下说实话：少爷所往皆是那小旦琴言处，见面多是哭；五月里说往怡园，其实是同两个小旦游运河；前日在那小旦家得了一个纸包，回来就成了这个样子。夫人又问李元茂，元茂只说去年常听魏聘才夸赞琴言，正月初六会馆团拜看戏之后便留心起来，余事须问魏聘才。于是差许顺往华府相请。聘才见夫人愁容，先说了些江西家信、场期高中的套话，及听说病源，一时良心发动，脸上竟露愧色；随即改口自表功劳：他正因世兄为一个小旦荒废诗书，才怂慂华公子买他，如今人已入府，从此可以杜绝痴心。夫人反倒谢他，又求他设法叫琴言出来一见。聘才便层层作难：那师傅在钱眼里过日子，一年二千四百银的进项，非钱不行。夫人心痛儿子，许了二百两。聘才回府却另有一副面孔：他知华公子的脾气断不能明说，只死缠林珊枝，许他两匹新花样库纱。珊枝原有自己的心事——琴言进来半月，连华夫人都疼他，常赏东西，说这孩子老实不像唱戏的；珊枝进府三年，恐怕被他压下去，正想设法挤他，因此一口应承，只说是自己作主，不许提聘才。次日恰值华公子身子不爽，在夫人房里不出来。琴言听说子玉病重，眼泪汪汪，随聘才重门叠户地出来，坐在放下帘子的车里，四儿坐车尾。到梅宅径入书房，许顺诧异而不敢拦。颜夫人一见琴言便吃一惊：玉骨冰肌，华光丽质，梳了头便是千金小姐，竟像未过门的媳妇王琼华，本来一肚子怒气，不觉化作笑容。琴言战战兢兢磕头，如实供说：苏州人，父母双亡，被族中叔母卖出，去年冬天到京，在联锦班；正月初六姑苏会馆唱戏，第一次见着少爷，就像从前认识一般；元宵在怡园看灯，最爱那"落花人独立，微雨燕双飞"的灯谜，徐子云以一张瑶琴为彩，说谁猜着便请谁相见，恰是少爷猜着；隔两日请他吃酒，才算相识；第二次便是素兰邀游运河——半年之内统共两面。夫人听得称奇，说既如此，何以他心心念念只是你？琴言道，不但小的觉得像旧识，少爷见了也这样说，彼此都说不出缘故。夫人反生怜意，命他进房去劝慰，自与聘才坐在外间静听，许顺也跟到卧房外细听。`,
    en: `Beaten and out of choices, Cao Changqing goes with Ye Maolin to Wei Pincai's study in the Hua mansion to beg pardon, offering to deliver the boy at once and take not a penny. Pincai, certain his own display of temper produced this, sends word through Lin Shanzhi. Hua Guangsu asks why no price is wanted; because, says Pincai, the master fears the boy does not know the rules and wishes to be able to take him back. Hua consents, but rules that once inside these walls, bought or not, Qinyan may never again pour wine or sing outside, and orders the counting-house to pay Changqing two hundred taels a month. Du Qinyan knows there is nowhere left to hide. He longs to see Mei Ziyu once, yet fears the pain of it and the many watching eyes; he tries to write and finds his mind in pieces and ten thousand words refusing the brush. At last he takes the silk handkerchief he has so often dried his tears with, wraps four medicines inside it, and gives it to Lu Sulan to deliver. Next day he enters the mansion; Hua studies him with great satisfaction and installs him in the Liuqing Study, then brings him to Su Huanxiang, who, seeing a slight and graceful figure without a trace of actorly manner, says one word: "Good." Qinyan weeps in private, and after some days, finding his new master's conduct correct enough, is a little reassured — though before Wei Pincai he keeps the silence of Lady Xi. Ziyu, calling at Sulan's, finds Wang Lanbao and Yuan Qiguan there. Sulan brings out the unlabelled parcel; the handkerchief falls open and four medicines roll out. Ziyu, taking them for a dying man's keepsake, nearly collapses, and Sulan hurriedly assures him that Yunong is alive, then tells the whole story: Pincai's offer, Xi Shiyi's rampage, the council of friends, the flight by night, the delivery to the Hua mansion. The drugs are morning glory, angelica, peony and stephania, identified by Qiguan's servant, who once worked in a pharmacy. Sulan reads "angelica" — literally living alone — as separation; Lanbao reads "stephania," which is written as guarding oneself, as a promise of chastity; but Ziyu construes the whole rebus: the peony's other name means about to part, so the parting is done; parted, he can only live alone; living alone behind those walls, he will guard himself like jade; and the morning glory, the Herdboy star, can only mean the seventh night of the seventh month, when Herdboy and Weaving Maid meet. Sulan claps and engages him to stay the night of the festival. Qiguan, meeting Ziyu for the first time, admires him greatly, and tells him how vilely Pincai behaved on the boat north, then curses Xi Shiyi through his teeth. Ziyu goes home aching in every limb and by the next day is gravely ill — speech disordered, weeping several times a day, calling in his sleep for Yunong, for Xiangwan, and cursing Xi Shiyi and Wei Pincai. Madam Yan, supposing him bewitched, tries physicians, divination and vows for ten days together with no effect; Yan Zhongqing and Wang Xun visit often, understand perfectly, and can do nothing. Suspecting "Yunong" to be a woman, she questions Yun'er, who denies everything until the rod is fetched and then kneels and tells all: that his master's outings were to the young actor Qinyan, that their meetings were mostly weeping, that the errand to Yiyuan in the fifth month was really a day on the canal, and that a paper parcel received at the actor's house had brought on this state. Li Yuanmao adds only that Pincai used to praise Qinyan, and that Ziyu grew attentive after the guild-hall gathering on the sixth of the first month. So Xu Shun is sent to fetch Pincai. Confronted with the mother's grief, Pincai's conscience stirs and shame shows in his face; then he recovers and claims the credit — seeing his friend ruining health and studies over an actor, he had urged Hua Guangsu to buy the boy, and now that the boy is immured the infatuation can die. Madam Yan thanks him, and begs him to contrive a visit. Pincai piles up difficulties: the master lives inside the eye of a coin and draws two thousand four hundred a year; nothing moves without money. Aching for her son, she promises two hundred taels. Back at the mansion he wears another face entirely. Knowing his master's temper makes an open request impossible, he works on Lin Shanzhi instead, promising two bolts of new-patterned silk. Shanzhi has his own reason: in half a month Qinyan has been petted even by Madam Hua, who gives him presents and calls him honest and unlike an actor, and Shanzhi, three years in the house, means to squeeze out a rival. So he agrees, on condition that the responsibility be called his own and Pincai's name never mentioned. The next morning Hua is unwell and keeps to his wife's room. Hearing that Ziyu is dangerously ill, Qinyan follows Pincai out through gate after gate with tears standing in his eyes, riding inside the carriage with the blind down and Si'er at the back. At the Mei house Pincai walks straight to the study; Xu Shun, astonished, dares not stop them. At first sight of Qinyan, Madam Yan is startled: jade bones and ice flesh, a bearing so noble that dressed as a woman he would pass for a great house's daughter — indeed he resembles her son's betrothed, Wang Qionghua — and her stored anger turns unaccountably into a smile. Trembling, Qinyan kowtows and answers truthfully: a Suzhou boy, both parents dead, sold off by a clan aunt, come to the capital last winter with the Lianjin troupe. On the sixth of the first month he sang at the Suzhou Guild Hall and saw the young master for the first time, and it was as though they had known each other before. At the Lantern Festival he went to Yiyuan to see the riddles, and loved best the one on the couplet about a man standing alone amid falling blossom and swallows flying in pairs through fine rain; Xu Ziyun set a jade qin as its prize and promised to introduce whoever solved it, and the solver was the young master; two days later he was invited to drink and Qinyan called to attend. That was their meeting; the second was Sulan's day on the canal. Twice in half a year, and no more. Madam Yan wonders aloud that so little should breed so much, and Qinyan answers that it was not only he who felt they had met before — the young master said the same, and neither of them could explain it. Pity replaces suspicion. She sends him in to console her son, and sits outside with Pincai to listen; Xu Shun, who has understood the case only now, follows to the bedroom door to listen too.`,
  },
  29: {
    zh: `杜琴言进梅宅时满拟要受一场凌辱，不料颜夫人非但不加呵叱，反有怜恤之意，还命他去劝慰。到卧房时但见帘帏不卷、几案生尘、药鼎烟浓、香炉灰烬，楠木小床垂着白绡帐。梅子玉半睡半醒，口中先念《长恨歌》的"七月七日长生殿，夜半无人私语时"——原来他梦中已是七夕，正与琴言在素兰处煮茗谈心；继而叹"黄泉碧落两难寻"，又惦记大雨与相隔甚远的船；末了竟说出一句"独活、防己之下，应须添一味当归"，把那个哑谜答了回去。颜夫人隔帘听着，两泪交流。琴言泪落满他一脸，几次低唤，子玉却冷笑道"你已试过了我一回，难道我还认不得你"，只当又是一个假琴言。魏聘才叫云儿绞了热手巾替他净面，扶起靠着叠好的锦被；颜夫人不肯进房，怕儿子愧惧。琴言把身子挪向亮处，侧脸相对，子玉这才心头一跳，问了又问，直到听见"真真是真的"，忽然狂笑一声扑在他肩上，笑个不住，满屋的人都笑了。琴言为治他的心病，破例说了些谎：说华公子极正经，府中上下待他都好，不用唱戏，不过在留青精舍当个伺候书房的书童；说太太已准他常来，还说等老爷回来要商量买他进来服侍少爷。子玉素知他不说谎，听了便四平八稳，欢喜得手舞足蹈，跨下床走了两步，说自己不过受了些暑气，又叫云儿拿东西来吃。琴言临别再三叮嘱，子玉也劝他从此与聘才和好，又嘱他见太太时说话须留意。颜夫人在外看得又惊又叹：这一对小人儿一个哭一个笑，并没说什么，若不是亲眼所见，任谁都要胡猜。她命许顺秤了二百两银子交与聘才——那正是聘才向她讹来的谎账，回府便锁进钱柜；又赏琴言银镶小刀、大小荷包、罗帕、洋表、梅花小锭与金面牙扇。回府时门上大簿子已记了一笔。林珊枝先吓唬他们，说公子传了两三回不见人，已差人去长庆家查问；待琴言急得落泪、聘才连连作揖，才笑说早已打点停当，叫那人城外逛一逛回来复命就是。随即又勒索起来：说那两匹库纱花样不好，他新近在沙回子处看中一个二两重的金丝花篮和一把嵌翡翠盖的时大彬宜兴壶——那壶倒茶不滴一点，不放茶叶冲下开水也是淡绿好茶——请聘才买来奉送，连门簿上那一笔也求他抹去。到了留青精舍，珊枝又追问琴言：无缘无故怎会害病？见你戏的又不止他一个。说得琴言臊得无可回答。聘才却把这段奇闻在城外各处传扬，各班相公添枝加叶，越传越不成话。王文辉在席上从蓉官、二喜口中听见，心里老大不快：子玉才十八岁，正是好花含蕊的时候，偏偏去年将爱女许了他，有了外遇，将来琴瑟便难专好。他盘问王恂，王恂含糊遮掩；陆氏夫人却先替女儿吃起醋来，把子玉数落一顿，连王文辉也带累挨了训。王琼华听母亲说了又说，低头不语，只想着"才人行短"，转念又记起哥哥、姊夫都称子玉正经多情，或者他所爱的不过是那份颜色罢了，便红了脸，手拈衣带呆坐出神。子玉自琴言来后不到十日精神复旧，只是见了母亲总有些惶恐；颜夫人体贴，绝口不提琴言。他终究放心不下，借看聘才为名又去了一趟，由珊枝把琴言叫出来谈了一个多时辰。华府人多嘴杂，此事很快传到华光宿耳中。公子动了气，叫珊枝上来责问几句，从此不准琴言出门，把他派到洗红居，交十珠婢看管，不与外人通问，与拘禁无异。幸而众婢多情爱好，倒着实照应；而琴言年纪虽小，性情异样，守身如玉，连十珠婢也防着，公子看他老诚，索性把他当个丫鬟看待。至此莫说走出来，连林珊枝不奉呼唤也进不去。琴言日间与众婢略说几句，夜来只独守孤灯，怨那秋风秋雨而已。`,
    en: `Du Qinyan enters the Mei house braced for humiliation, and instead finds Madam Yan not only forbearing but kind, and is sent in to console the invalid. In the sickroom the curtains hang undrawn, dust lies on the desk, the medicine tripod smokes, the censer is all ash, and a small nanmu bed is veiled in white gauze. Mei Ziyu, half asleep, is murmuring lines from the *Song of Everlasting Sorrow* — the seventh night of the seventh month in the Hall of Long Life, the hour when no one overhears — for in his dream the festival has already come and he sits brewing tea with Qinyan at Sulan's; then he sighs that neither the Yellow Springs nor the blue void will yield her up; then frets about the downpour and the boat lying so far off; and at last, answering the herb-rebus in his sleep, says that after angelica and stephania there ought to be added one dose of danggui — the herb whose name means ought to return. Listening beyond the curtain, Madam Yan weeps. Qinyan's tears fall over the sleeper's face, and when he calls, Ziyu only laughs coldly: you tested me once already, do you think I would not know you — taking him for another counterfeit. Wei Pincai has Yun'er wring out a hot cloth, wipes his face, and props him against folded brocade; Madam Yan will not come in, lest her son be shamed. Qinyan shifts toward the light and turns his face, and Ziyu's heart jumps; he asks and asks again, and when he hears "truly, it is truly true," he bursts into wild laughter and pitches forward onto Qinyan's shoulder, laughing until the whole room laughs with him. To cure the sickness Qinyan tells his first lies: that Hua Guangsu is entirely correct, that everyone in the mansion treats him well, that he need not sing but serves in the Liuqing Study like a study-boy, that Madam Yan has licensed him to come often, and that when the master returns there is talk of buying him to wait on the young gentleman. Knowing that Qinyan never lies, Ziyu is settled past all shaking; he climbs down, takes two steps, declares it was only a touch of summer heat, and calls for something to eat. At parting Qinyan urges him earnestly, and Ziyu urges him in turn to be reconciled with Pincai, and to weigh his words before Madam Yan. Outside, she marvels: one weeping, one laughing, and nothing said between them — had she not seen it herself, anyone would have imagined the worst. She has Xu Shun weigh out two hundred taels for Pincai, the sum he has extorted with a lie, which goes straight into his cash chest at the mansion; and she gives Qinyan a silver-mounted knife, purses large and small, a handkerchief, a foreign watch, small plum-blossom ingots, and an ivory fan with gold leaf. At the gate their return is entered in the register. Lin Shanzhi first frightens them — the young lord called for Qinyan three times and has sent a man to Changqing's to verify the story — and only when Qinyan is in tears and Pincai bowing does he laugh and admit he has already told the man to stroll about outside the city and report that it was indeed the master's birthday. Then he collects his price: the two bolts of silk are the wrong pattern, but at Sha the Muslim's he has seen a gold-wire flower basket of two taels' weight and a Shi Dabin Yixing teapot with a kingfisher-jade inset lid — a pot that pours without a drop spilt and yields pale-green tea from plain boiling water — and Pincai may buy him both, and have the register entry erased besides. Back at the Liuqing Study, Shanzhi presses Qinyan: how does a man fall ill over nothing, when others see you act and do not sicken? — and Qinyan, scarlet, has no answer. Pincai meanwhile publishes the wonder all over the outer city, and rival actors embroider it out of recognition. Wang Wenhui hears the tale from Rongguan and Er Xi at a banquet and is deeply displeased: Ziyu is eighteen, a bud not yet open, and only last year he promised his favourite daughter to him; an outside attachment bodes ill for the harmony of the marriage. He questions Wang Xun, who answers evasively and defends his cousin, and remains unsatisfied. Madam Lu is jealous on her daughter's behalf before the girl is even married, and delivers a lecture that catches her own husband in its sweep. Wang Qionghua, hearing it repeated, sits with lowered head, thinking that a man of talent is short on conduct — then remembering that her brother and brother-in-law both call Ziyu upright and deeply feeling, and wondering whether it is only beauty he loves, she blushes, twists her sash, and stares at nothing. Ziyu is himself again within ten days, though shy before his mother, who never once mentions Qinyan. Still uneasy, he uses a call on Pincai as pretext and goes again, and Shanzhi brings Qinyan out to him for above an hour. In a house of so many mouths the story soon reaches Hua Guangsu, who is angry, rebukes Shanzhi, forbids Qinyan the gate, and moves him to the Xihong Lodge under the eyes of the ten pearl-maids, cut off from every outside message — imprisonment in all but name. The maids, being warm-hearted, look after him kindly, and another boy would have thought it paradise; but Qinyan, young and singular, guards himself like jade even among them, so that Hua, satisfied of his gravity, treats him as one more maid and forbids only the outside world. Now there is no walking out, and even Lin Shanzhi cannot come unsummoned. By day Qinyan exchanges a few words with the maids; by night he keeps a solitary lamp and quarrels with the autumn wind and rain.`,
  },
  30: {
    zh: `杜琴言擅自出府一事，华光宿虽未究其细底，只责他不知规矩，姑免其罚，却把他拨到内室，从此里外不通。他独坐水晶山畔，对着几丛凤仙垂泪：花在本根相偎相倚，自有天然机趣，纵有风吹雨打也无损根本；一被折入金盆玉盎，剪枝摘叶、绳拴线缚，合了人的眼睛，减却花的颜色，与把人拘禁得笑不敢笑、哭不敢哭何异？何况人还不如花——花今年谢了明年再开，人却一年不似一年；他日被人厌恶时，便如落花之飘茵落圂，沾泥带水，无所归结。又想到自己那日为宽子玉之心撒的谎，倘教他知道如今音信断绝，只怕旧病复发。八月十二，丹桂满院，华公子记起六月在怡园许下的秋凉之约，因十五是家宴，便定十四夜请徐子云、萧次贤、刘文泽，并声明夜宴。是时正值秋闱二场：主考正是刘文泽之父大宗伯，副主考王文辉新迁学士，陆宗沅、杨方猷、周锡爵、孙亮功一齐分房，名士回避了大半；徐子云、史南湘已是前科举人，萧次贤高尚不仕，只有田春航、高品入场，梅子玉与王恂、刘文泽、颜仲清皆例应回避。子玉在家闷坐，琴言杳无音信，几次想去访聘才，又厌华府豪奴气焰，终于不去。西园之胜，原是老公爷的画友谢笠山经营十二年，浓淡疏密各得其宜；华公子爱繁华，改得一味雕琢绚烂，不留一分朴素。地方二十亩，一道玉带河六折六桥，长廊曲榭，岸边号"小苏堤"；假山两种，小者太湖石嵌空玲珑，大者黄石高至数丈，苍藤绿苔缠护。张仲雨一早进来，午后同魏聘才随林珊枝闲游：穿神仙洞、过丹桂，看凹字形七间的"归鸿小渚"和华公子的亲笔小跋；再上藏书楼，两所作丁字形，缥缃万卷，芸香袭人；又见壁挂瑶琴、几列彝鼎的水阁，湘竹几椅，退光漆面，仲雨叹此地洗尽繁华，珊枝说公子虽爱热闹，其实也喜清静；末了是白石平台上的玻璃水榭，云母屏风，一缸金鱼养着四尺高的水晶山。这日之宴分三处：日间恩庆堂演戏，酉戌之间小平山看杂技，入夜留青精舍演灯戏。恩庆堂上五大名班合演，随后袁宝珠、苏蕙芳、陆素兰、金漱芳、李玉林、王兰保、王桂保、林春喜、袁琪官等九人添上全福班一个，合演《秦淮河看花大会》，幽闲妖冶各极其致；华公子笑说这是抄袭子云旧文，子云却赞没有一人雷同，萧次贤因说如今秦淮冷落，马湘兰的相貌也不过中等。子云不见琴言，问起，公子只说他脾气虽古怪却老实，如今派在内书房。及至十旦上来敬酒，子云问苏蕙芳田春航的场中文字，文泽便揭他天天焚香祷告；华公子追问，蕙芳斟酒相拦，张仲雨又笑他"事急借人作护身符，如今倒忘恩负义"，说得蕙芳脸红，华公子想起前情，动了怜念，便把话岔开。饭后众人穿廊过院，壁上一块六尺高的祝枝山草书木刻，跟班一推竟是暗门，门后绿阴满目、水声潺潺。琴言这才由珊枝领出来见客：低眉垂首，全无从前高傲神气，隔了两月，倒像相逢陌路；子云问了几句，几番要问子玉，终于缩住。看过像声、《龙图公案》与变戏法，月色将上，红灯十数对引路，五彩绸坊悬起数百盏画灯，庭心八枝锡地照打成花卉，火树银花一般。饮到二更，冯子佩忽然半夜闯来，说今日随妻舅归自荣到乌大傻家拜寿，公摊九十吊叫了全福班，归自荣串《独占》，自己却被乌大傻死缠去唱《活捉》——正做到进场时，刑部一张票子、一条铁链把乌大傻锁了去，满堂哭喊而散。张仲雨一猜便着：乌大傻造假房契替归自荣借了六百吊，钱是两人分用，如今独他倒运。华公子劝子佩登台，子佩说不是相公巴结谁，子云说顾曲登场原是风流自赏；及至配戏，聘才所会的《折柳》《赏荷》《小宴》《琴挑》《偷诗》，子佩一概不会，他会的却是《前诱》《后诱》《反诳》《挑帘》《裁衣》一路，末了还是八龄班一个孩子低声提起，才定了《活捉》。子佩出场竟幽怨可怜，喑呜如泣，轻云随足、淡烟抹袖，纤音摇曳得灯火为之不明，众人如不相识，华公子离席赞他"居然像个好妇人"。此时刘文泽与蕙芳低语的却不是子佩，而是归自荣：此人江西籍而寄籍直隶，进过一名秀才，少年标致，在京八年不归，谎称断弦娶了乌大傻之妹，又纳叶茂林之女为副室，别居城南；叶女奁资颇厚，两年花尽，夫妻交谪，她便倚门卖俏，自荣先怒后安，竟明目张胆当起忘八，还雇了伙计在家，而口中犹常称某给事为业师。华公子不喜与闻这些，只顾看戏出神，忽命捧上大玉杯，送到子云面前。`,
    en: `Hua Guangsu never inquires into the details of Du Qinyan's unlicensed outing; he merely charges him with not knowing the rules, waives the punishment as a first offence, and moves him to the inner apartments, where nothing passes in or out. Sitting alone by the crystal rockery, Qinyan weeps over a clump of balsam. Flowers left leaning on one another at the root have a natural life, and wind and rain hurt only the season, not the stock; but plucked into a golden basin, pruned, stripped, bound with cord to please a human eye, they lose the very colour that was praised — how is that different from penning a person up until he dares neither laugh nor cry? And people are worse off than flowers, for a flower that falls this year opens again the next, while a person only declines; and when he is at last found tiresome he will be a fallen petal blown into the mire, soiled and belonging nowhere. He thinks too of the lies he told to quiet Ziyu, and fears that the silence now closing over them will bring the illness back. By the twelfth of the eighth month the courtyard is heavy with cassia. Remembering the promise made at Yiyuan in the sixth month, and the family feast falling on the fifteenth, Hua fixes the fourteenth for Xu Ziyun, Xiao Cixian, and Liu Wenze, specifying a night banquet. It is the second session of the autumn examinations: the chief examiner is Liu Wenze's own father, the Minister of Rites, the deputy is Wang Wenhui, newly raised to Academician, and Lu Zongyuan, Yang Fangyou, Zhou Xijue, and Sun Lianggong all sit as section examiners — so half the known scholars must withdraw. Xu Ziyun and Shi Nanxiang already hold their degree, Xiao Cixian holds himself above office, and only Tian Chunhang and Gao Pin go in, while Mei Ziyu, Wang Xun, Wenze, and Yan Zhongqing are barred by kinship. Ziyu sits at home with no word of Qinyan, thinks more than once of calling on Pincai, and cannot face the insolence of the Hua servants. The West Garden was laid out over twelve years by the old duke's painter friend Xie Lishan, light and dense in right proportion, until Hua Guangsu, loving splendour, reworked it into unbroken carving and colour with no plainness left. Twenty mu; the Jade Belt River winding through six bends under six bridges; long galleries and water pavilions; a bank called the Little Su Embankment; rockeries of two kinds, small ones of fretted Taihu stone and great yellow-stone crags tens of feet high, netted with old vine and moss. Zhang Zhongyu comes early, and after noon tours the place with Wei Pincai behind Lin Shanzhi: through a grotto and past cassias to the seven-bay Returning Geese Islet with Hua's own inscription; up into the library towers, two blocks set like the character for T, thousands of volumes breathing the scent of insect-repelling herb; then a water pavilion hung with jade qins and ranked with ancient bronzes, its furniture of speckled bamboo under dull lacquer, of which Zhongyu says that here all splendour is washed away, and Shanzhi answers that his master loves quiet as well as noise; and last a glass-topped summer pavilion on a white stone terrace, with mica screens and a great jar of goldfish holding a crystal hill four feet high. The day has three settings: plays at Enqing Hall, acrobatics at Xiaopingshan toward evening, lantern plays at the Liuqing Study by night. Five great troupes perform; then nine of the famous ten — Yuan Baozhu, Su Huifang, Lu Sulan, Jin Shufang, Li Yulin, Wang Lanbao, Wang Guibao, Lin Chunxi, Yuan Qiguan — with one of the Quanfu troupe added, give the *Flower-Viewing Assembly on the Qinhuai*, secluded grace and open coquetry each carried to its limit. Hua calls it a rehash of Ziyun's old piece; Ziyun answers that no two of them are alike; and Xiao Cixian observes that the Qinhuai is desolate now, and that even Ma Xianglan's looks were middling. Missing Qinyan, Ziyun asks after him, and is told only that the boy's temper is odd but honest and that he now serves in the inner study. When the ten come up to pour wine, Ziyun asks Su Huifang how Tian Chunhang's examination essays went, and Wenze betrays that Huifang burns incense and prays for him daily; Hua presses for the story, Huifang blocks Ziyun with a cup, and Zhang Zhongyu laughs that having once used a great name as a shield he is now ungrateful — at which Huifang colours, and Hua, remembering something, feels a stir of pity and lets the subject drop. Afterwards the party threads corridors and courts to a six-foot panel of Zhu Zhishan's cursive set in a wall, which an attendant pushes open: it is a hidden door, and beyond it green shade and the sound of water. Only here does Shanzhi bring Qinyan out — eyes lowered, all his old pride gone, so that after two months he and Ziyun meet almost as strangers; Ziyun asks him a few things, twice moves to ask about Ziyu, and each time stops. After vocal mimicry, a recital from the *Cases of the Dragon Diagram*, and conjurers, the moon rises; a dozen pairs of red lanterns lead them out beneath a five-coloured silk archway hung with hundreds of painted lanterns, past eight pewter standing lamps wrought into flowers with the light inside the blossoms, fire-trees and silver flowers. They drink to the second watch, and then Feng Zipei bursts in at midnight with his story: he had gone with his brother-in-law Gui Zirong to a birthday at Wu Dasha's, where ninety strings were pooled for the Quanfu troupe and Gui performed *Exclusive Possession*, while Wu badgered Feng into playing *Capturing Alive* with him — and at the very moment of the capture, men came in with a warrant from the Board of Punishments and led Wu away in chains, the women wailing and the party scattering. Zhang Zhongyu guesses at once: Wu forged a house deed to raise six hundred strings for Gui, the two split the money, and now Wu alone pays. Hua urges Feng onto the stage; Feng retorts that he is no actor with anyone to flatter, and Ziyun answers that treading the boards is a pleasure taken for its own sake. The repertories will not meet: Pincai offers *Plucking a Willow Branch*, *Appreciating the Lotuses*, *The Small Banquet*, *Flirting with a Zither*, *Stealing the Poem*, and Feng knows none of them, his own being *Prior Enticement*, *Later Enticement*, *Counter-Deception*, *Lifting the Curtain*, and *Cutting Cloth* — until a boy of the Baling troupe murmurs that Pincai has been seen in *Capturing Alive*, and that is settled on. Feng's performance astonishes them: plaintive and sobbing, light cloud at his feet and thin mist brushed from his sleeves, a thread of sound that seems to dim the lamps, so that Hua leaves his seat to say that the man is truly like a beautiful woman. Meanwhile Liu Wenze and Huifang, whispering, are not discussing Feng at all but Gui Zirong — a Jiangxi man registered in Zhili who took a licentiate's degree, handsome, eight years in the capital without going home, who pretended his wife had died in order to marry Wu Dasha's sister, then took Ye Maolin's daughter as a second wife in a separate house south of the city; her dowry was large and gone in two years, and when poverty set them quarrelling she began displaying herself at the door, old acquaintances resumed their visits, and Gui, angry at first and then finding the money easy, kept the trade openly and even hired an assistant, though he still speaks of a Supervising Secretary as his teacher. Hua dislikes such talk and ignores it, absorbed in the stage, and calls for a great jade goblet to be carried to Ziyun.`,
  },
};
